1
00:00:04,422 --> 00:00:08,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Practical side of it being what?

2
00:00:08,345 --> 00:00:10,387
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

3
00:00:24,374 --> 00:00:30,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of the time, when practice is talked about, it's just sort of talked about and sort of entertained as a thought.

4
00:00:30,979 --> 00:00:45,148
[SPEAKER_01]: But on a day-to-day sort of living of dhamma, then it's sort of like... it's very easy to revert to keeping things in an unannounced length.

5
00:00:45,629 --> 00:00:46,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, sure.

6
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:52,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the... that's like the... Hey, Dalia.

7
00:00:55,243 --> 00:01:02,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it needs to be recognized even before you become a monk.

8
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:13,415
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that the dhamma, in order to be done properly, requires a great deal of giving up.

9
00:01:13,535 --> 00:01:20,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, theoretically, a lay person can practice the dhamma, but from the dhamma point of view, lay life, it's already a compromise.

10
00:01:22,657 --> 00:01:28,802
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a compromise for somebody who is unaware of the dhamma, and unaware of the requirements, and unaware of what the practice is.

11
00:01:29,562 --> 00:01:37,429
[SPEAKER_00]: But for somebody who is aware of it, who kind of got the idea, and then chooses to stay a layman, that's a compromise.

12
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:43,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly already the beginning of keeping the dhamma at the arm's length.

13
00:01:44,074 --> 00:01:46,636
[SPEAKER_00]: So you know where the dhamma should apply,

14
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:54,230
[SPEAKER_00]: But, for whatever reasons, personal, you refuse to go in that direction.

15
00:01:54,570 --> 00:02:04,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you choose to focus then on the suttas and the instances where the Buddha sort of did successfully enable the right view in lay people.

16
00:02:07,381 --> 00:02:09,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, oh, so I don't have to be a monk.

17
00:02:11,484 --> 00:02:12,325
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to give up

18
00:02:14,139 --> 00:02:14,739
[SPEAKER_00]: My existence.

19
00:02:14,979 --> 00:02:17,161
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to give up familiarity of my environment.

20
00:02:17,321 --> 00:02:19,984
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to give up my family, and I can get the dhamma.

21
00:02:21,465 --> 00:02:26,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, those people were far, far less in number back in the Buddha's day.

22
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:29,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Like lay people who became sotāpannas.

23
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:33,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Mostly it was monks and nuns.

24
00:02:34,296 --> 00:02:36,618
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no instance of a lay person being an anārahant.

25
00:02:38,039 --> 00:02:39,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's impossible to live that life.

26
00:02:42,496 --> 00:02:46,838
[SPEAKER_00]: With a full enlightened mind, the environment, the values, the whole thing, it's impossible.

27
00:02:48,859 --> 00:02:55,561
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it always boils down to this kind of your actions and your choices and lack of transparency in regard to it.

28
00:02:59,103 --> 00:03:01,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's obvious, not everybody can be a monk.

29
00:03:03,144 --> 00:03:08,987
[SPEAKER_00]: But, everybody is perfectly capable of not avoiding responsibility for that.

30
00:03:14,592 --> 00:03:17,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the lack of transparency.

31
00:03:18,394 --> 00:03:20,875
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's mostly where I was getting at.

32
00:03:22,816 --> 00:03:35,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, see, even when you do commit, it's, after a while, it's very easy to feel fully justified to be... Oh, yeah, because becoming a monk

33
00:03:37,784 --> 00:03:43,610
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a necessary step towards creating the circumstances for the practice.

34
00:03:43,770 --> 00:03:47,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not... doesn't mean automatically you're practicing by becoming a monk.

35
00:03:48,254 --> 00:03:50,296
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, that's another thing sometimes people need to realize.

36
00:03:50,316 --> 00:03:51,897
[SPEAKER_00]: They think, oh, if only I'll become a monk.

37
00:03:52,618 --> 00:03:52,978
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

38
00:03:53,078 --> 00:03:53,979
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all I need to do.

39
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:55,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I'll be fine.

40
00:03:56,181 --> 00:03:56,862
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not at all.

41
00:03:57,302 --> 00:03:58,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still a layman, basically.

42
00:03:59,264 --> 00:04:00,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're a monk.

43
00:04:00,605 --> 00:04:01,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you become a sotāpanna.

44
00:04:01,926 --> 00:04:02,987
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only difference.

45
00:04:03,864 --> 00:04:08,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Between a layman and a member of the saṅgha.

46
00:04:09,548 --> 00:04:17,093
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you wearing brown cloth wrapped around you is just a new fashion statement.

47
00:04:18,734 --> 00:04:20,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you get the right view.

48
00:04:20,455 --> 00:04:21,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a statement.

49
00:04:21,156 --> 00:04:22,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, look, I'm this.

50
00:04:22,937 --> 00:04:24,038
[SPEAKER_00]: This is my set of values.

51
00:04:24,058 --> 00:04:27,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a necessary statement, more often than not.

52
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:31,223
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean you arrive at clarity, transparency and so on.

53
00:04:32,963 --> 00:04:37,446
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to always keep the reasons for becoming a monk in the first place, keep it in your mind.

54
00:04:37,526 --> 00:04:37,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

55
00:04:38,027 --> 00:04:38,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did you want to become a monk?

56
00:04:39,828 --> 00:04:41,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you want to be free from suffering.

57
00:04:41,289 --> 00:04:43,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you free from suffering simply by becoming a monk?

58
00:04:43,511 --> 00:04:43,671
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

59
00:04:44,852 --> 00:04:47,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so now you can actually practice further.

60
00:04:49,615 --> 00:05:00,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's when people forget that, that's when, you know, you becoming a monk becomes more like a career, socially oriented purposes and so on.

61
00:05:01,728 --> 00:05:05,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is all nice in the worldly manner, but it's a... it's a layman's undertaking.

62
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:16,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if, you know, if somebody, like, doesn't want to practice dharma, chooses to be a layman, that's absolutely fine.

63
00:05:20,191 --> 00:05:23,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can't... Well, you can't complain about anything.

64
00:05:23,572 --> 00:05:24,812
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a different topic.

65
00:05:24,992 --> 00:05:29,473
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... the problem is when... when you know the value of the dhamma,

66
00:05:31,074 --> 00:05:39,060
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what it entails, at least to an extent, but then you choose to justify not doing it because, you know, you need to be a layman.

67
00:05:39,861 --> 00:05:41,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's a compromise.

68
00:05:42,002 --> 00:05:45,605
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you kind of disqualify yourself from proper understanding of the dhamma.

69
00:05:50,049 --> 00:05:54,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you say, like, keep an eye out for your anxieties?

70
00:05:54,512 --> 00:05:55,733
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a good way to...

71
00:05:56,153 --> 00:05:57,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, keep an eye for the suffering.

72
00:05:58,476 --> 00:06:08,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all of these acts of covering things up from yourself, it's to cover up the responsibility, because it's felt unpleasantly.

73
00:06:09,381 --> 00:06:19,405
[SPEAKER_00]: If by some miracle responsibility would be felt pleasantly, nobody would be covering it, and everybody would automatically go towards arahantship, towards full enlightenment.

74
00:06:20,886 --> 00:06:21,666
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the opposite.

75
00:06:22,526 --> 00:06:23,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Responsibility is not pleasant.

76
00:06:24,467 --> 00:06:25,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Never is.

77
00:06:25,187 --> 00:06:26,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Never was, never will be.

78
00:06:27,473 --> 00:06:28,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was never a problem.

79
00:06:29,095 --> 00:06:38,324
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is you not being able to endure that discomfort and habitually looking away from it, giving in to distracting yourself from it.

80
00:06:38,344 --> 00:06:44,350
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you do it, the more dependent you become on doing it, doing that avoidance.

81
00:06:45,731 --> 00:06:51,357
[SPEAKER_01]: That sort of meant like... using anxiety as a criteria?

82
00:06:52,503 --> 00:06:53,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

83
00:06:53,203 --> 00:06:59,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Using anxiety as a criteria is another way of saying using... discerning the liability to suffering as a criteria.

84
00:07:01,006 --> 00:07:03,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't matter if you're a layman, doesn't matter if you're a monk.

85
00:07:03,987 --> 00:07:05,928
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is, are you still liable to suffering?

86
00:07:08,949 --> 00:07:12,991
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's virtually inseparable.

87
00:07:13,031 --> 00:07:16,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Liability to suffering, it's virtually inseparable from being a layman.

88
00:07:17,963 --> 00:07:24,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, by proxy then, if you really want to commit to free yourself from suffering, you have to recognize you need to commit to changing your environment.

89
00:07:25,628 --> 00:07:37,815
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot live in the world around... that's fully based upon values of sensuality, family, company, distraction, desires, lack of restraint, and somehow go beyond that.

90
00:07:43,798 --> 00:07:46,340
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, you can say, oh, it's not my fault.

91
00:07:47,357 --> 00:07:48,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I'm not a monk.

92
00:07:48,097 --> 00:07:48,718
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't be a monk.

93
00:07:48,978 --> 00:07:50,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can say it's not my fault that I have to be a layman.

94
00:07:51,419 --> 00:07:56,822
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is your fault that you're still liable to suffering.

95
00:07:56,842 --> 00:07:57,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter what you are.

96
00:07:58,523 --> 00:08:00,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Your fault is whether you're liable to suffering or not.

97
00:08:00,804 --> 00:08:00,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

98
00:08:01,044 --> 00:08:05,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Because suffering is nothing to do with any external circumstance whatsoever.

99
00:08:05,446 --> 00:08:12,050
[SPEAKER_00]: If you suffer, it's because there is craving in your mind, not in the things that made you suffer, seemingly.

100
00:08:13,212 --> 00:08:14,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something you're always responsible for.

101
00:08:15,454 --> 00:08:23,380
[SPEAKER_00]: But if, as a rule of thumb, you avoid responsibility for even things that are not so deeply rooted, you're not going to see this.

102
00:08:24,161 --> 00:08:33,288
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you start accepting responsibility for everything else, in order to... in order to be able to see it on this level.

103
00:08:33,308 --> 00:08:37,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being a monk is basically... it gives you a sort of a better environment, better focus.

104
00:08:38,356 --> 00:08:40,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, not by default.

105
00:08:40,238 --> 00:08:52,109
[SPEAKER_00]: You still need to exercise certain, you know, reasoned decisions, because, you know, some of the monasteries are simply not suitable.

106
00:08:52,810 --> 00:08:54,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of the places are not suitable.

107
00:08:56,093 --> 00:08:58,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not being a monk in any tradition is not suitable.

108
00:08:59,450 --> 00:09:10,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be a monk, and people will think it's the same Buddhist monk in the tradition that doesn't keep vinaya, that simply doesn't have the precepts, or has completely different precepts that are not on the same level.

109
00:09:12,037 --> 00:09:24,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to be... and I'm not saying like only to be in the right tradition, but you need to be in the right set of values, which means you need to know the set of values, and you read the suttas, and you get the set of values.

110
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:30,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yes, you have like Theravāda monks, which is what we are.

111
00:09:31,535 --> 00:09:34,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Many of them would not keep the rules that you're supposed to keep.

112
00:09:36,918 --> 00:09:38,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's just how things are.

113
00:09:38,859 --> 00:09:39,719
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not your problem.

114
00:09:42,961 --> 00:09:44,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't mean like a whole Theravāda... No.

115
00:09:45,723 --> 00:09:54,027
[SPEAKER_00]: People individually choose to not adhere, not adhere to rules or things that they know should be adhered to.

116
00:09:56,231 --> 00:10:03,779
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can still choose to go to that tradition or some like other tradition that doesn't necessarily keep those rules, but then you can keep those rules, if you can.

117
00:10:05,461 --> 00:10:14,451
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you think that the environment will prevent you from keeping rules, because of, you know, what people would expect from you and so on, then you shouldn't be going into that environment.

118
00:10:16,427 --> 00:10:29,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I mean, ordaining in a Theravāda tradition, but in some temple monastery, that it's all about, like, you know, money and events and so on, well, that's not something you would want to do if you want to practice dhamma.

119
00:10:32,719 --> 00:10:38,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, precepts are a personal choice, a personal commitment, isn't it?

120
00:10:39,103 --> 00:10:40,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yeah, of course, yeah.

121
00:10:40,904 --> 00:10:44,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, again, comes through that recognition of personal responsibility.

122
00:10:46,563 --> 00:10:53,470
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no commandment that demands from you to do it, but if you want to practice the dhamma, there are necessary bases for it.

123
00:10:54,430 --> 00:10:55,471
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not optional.

124
00:10:56,733 --> 00:10:57,473
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's cheap.

125
00:10:57,954 --> 00:11:11,727
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be more honest if you say, I don't want to practice dhamma and I'll go and sort of not keep the precepts, than saying, I am practicing the dhamma and I don't need to keep the precepts, which is what you hear people relatively often say.

126
00:11:13,066 --> 00:11:18,888
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, you don't need to give up your lay life, you don't need to give up sensuality, and you can be enlightened.

127
00:11:20,489 --> 00:11:24,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not according to the Buddha, which, ironically, you profess to follow.

128
00:11:27,431 --> 00:11:34,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, today many things, vastly different things, are called Buddhism, but it doesn't mean that the Buddha was teaching all of that.

129
00:11:36,683 --> 00:11:40,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... if you wonder, so how do I know what the Buddha was teaching?

130
00:11:40,105 --> 00:11:50,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can't know a hundred percent, because he's long gone, but you can certainly know what is least likely to be wrong, in terms of the teachings.

131
00:11:51,573 --> 00:11:55,136
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can know what is most likely closest to what he's been teaching.

132
00:11:56,096 --> 00:11:58,538
[SPEAKER_00]: What all the traditions agree were the earliest texts.

133
00:12:00,637 --> 00:12:02,719
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how, okay, so this is my starting point.

134
00:12:02,919 --> 00:12:03,400
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not perfect.

135
00:12:03,420 --> 00:12:05,942
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be perfect, because it's been two and a half millennia since.

136
00:12:06,623 --> 00:12:07,844
[SPEAKER_00]: But this is my starting point.

137
00:12:08,705 --> 00:12:10,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll start investigating this.

138
00:12:11,888 --> 00:12:18,034
[SPEAKER_00]: But not like, I'll pick and choose that which will suit my already predetermined lifestyle that I do not want to change.

139
00:12:18,454 --> 00:12:18,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

140
00:12:18,714 --> 00:12:19,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm too afraid.

141
00:12:23,179 --> 00:12:25,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll know that you're covering something up,

142
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:49,719
[SPEAKER_00]: If you get emotionally disturbed on account of encountering the opposing view, if somebody says, you should... it's your duty to give up the lay life, and if you feel affected by that, or get angry with the person who said it, on top of that, you're covering things up.

143
00:12:49,779 --> 00:12:51,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you're a layman or because you're not a monk.

144
00:12:51,661 --> 00:12:53,723
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you're avoiding responsibility for what you are.

145
00:12:55,554 --> 00:12:56,315
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hard not to.

146
00:12:56,696 --> 00:12:57,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

147
00:12:57,597 --> 00:13:00,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why you are in that environment, in that setup.

148
00:13:01,441 --> 00:13:03,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

149
00:13:03,364 --> 00:13:11,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think that those days before, as a layperson myself, when I started reading the suttas, that, fortunately, became very clear to me.

150
00:13:14,458 --> 00:13:19,239
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha outline says that the only thing that matters in the world is suffering and freedom from it.

151
00:13:19,459 --> 00:13:21,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do, it's on account of how you feel.

152
00:13:21,999 --> 00:13:29,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Either to prolong pleasure, because not enough pleasure is suffering, or to avoid pain, because enduring pain is suffering.

153
00:13:31,161 --> 00:13:32,501
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, and this is the way out.

154
00:13:34,661 --> 00:13:35,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Come and see for yourself.

155
00:13:36,242 --> 00:13:36,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

156
00:13:37,962 --> 00:13:39,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remember reading that, and it was obvious.

157
00:13:39,902 --> 00:13:41,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to become a monk.

158
00:13:42,263 --> 00:13:43,683
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to give up the world.

159
00:13:44,735 --> 00:13:51,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, you know, there is suffering, there is the way out, you can follow it, but you don't have to.

160
00:13:51,857 --> 00:13:58,099
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not literally that I will be, like, enduring the eternal damnation if I don't become a monk.

161
00:13:59,059 --> 00:14:02,240
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, at the same time, I felt, oh, so now I'm responsible.

162
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:08,701
[SPEAKER_00]: For better, I go forth and become a monk and pursue this freedom from suffering that the Buddha teaches.

163
00:14:10,522 --> 00:14:12,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm equally responsible for not doing that.

164
00:14:12,563 --> 00:14:14,123
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I choose to stay a layman,

165
00:14:15,282 --> 00:14:24,385
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot complain for whatever happens to me for the rest of my life and future, whatever, wherever your beliefs were.

166
00:14:25,225 --> 00:14:36,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you might not be responsible for particular things, good or bad, that happen to you, but you're responsible for choosing to stay in the environment where these things can happen.

167
00:14:37,408 --> 00:14:39,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Environment being saṃsāra, fundamentally.

168
00:14:41,681 --> 00:14:44,444
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought, okay, so that's... it's quite an obvious choice.

169
00:14:44,544 --> 00:14:47,267
[SPEAKER_00]: And obviously, for me, it was a no-brainer choice.

170
00:14:48,608 --> 00:14:59,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Because really, like, the only... the only way you could stay in the environment that makes you liable, pressured by feelings, good or bad, doesn't matter, is to ignore that, that you are then liable.

171
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:02,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Which I couldn't ignore, because the choice became very clear.

172
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:14,385
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, becoming a monk felt like... not like a... some, you know, mystical duty that I need to do to be saved.

173
00:15:14,885 --> 00:15:18,967
[SPEAKER_00]: It just felt like, oh, it's just a step towards this right direction of freedom from suffering.

174
00:15:19,728 --> 00:15:25,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Just a step towards the environment that can provide me with better conditions for practice, more efficient practice.

175
00:15:26,951 --> 00:15:33,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Because most of the lay life, even if the one... if you want to practice the dhamma, would... most of your energy would be spent just sort of fending off

176
00:15:34,176 --> 00:15:37,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality and ill-will, and company, and crowd, and bombardment.

177
00:15:38,460 --> 00:15:49,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's no... there's no way you would, on top of that, then you'll make all the amount of... necessary amount of effort to actually discern through that muck and swamp of ignorance.

178
00:15:51,371 --> 00:15:58,458
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, if you don't spend most of your time just fending off things, then, yeah, you will have enough effort built up to start looking through things and... and deal with it.

179
00:15:59,599 --> 00:15:59,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

180
00:16:02,537 --> 00:16:06,018
[SPEAKER_01]: When people don't want to leave that sensuality and... Yeah.

181
00:16:06,359 --> 00:16:11,821
[SPEAKER_00]: See, and the only reason you wouldn't want to leave sensuality is because you still avoid responsibility for it.

182
00:16:13,541 --> 00:16:14,422
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to leave it.

183
00:16:15,042 --> 00:16:19,924
[SPEAKER_00]: But what you have to do is stop ignoring the fact that you are responsible for staying there.

184
00:16:19,944 --> 00:16:22,805
[SPEAKER_00]: When people say, yeah, oh, you're so lucky you can be a monk.

185
00:16:23,425 --> 00:16:27,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm... or we are lucky inasmuch we are still alive.

186
00:16:28,710 --> 00:16:30,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Having been, like, riddled with sickness and so on.

187
00:16:30,891 --> 00:16:31,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there is luck.

188
00:16:31,911 --> 00:16:33,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It's often pure chance.

189
00:16:33,392 --> 00:16:39,675
[SPEAKER_00]: A bit, you know, maybe we're a bit careful, make sure you kind of, you know, use your body, don't get indolent and whatever else.

190
00:16:40,475 --> 00:16:43,376
[SPEAKER_00]: But, overall, that's as far as the luck goes.

191
00:16:44,477 --> 00:16:49,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else is based on the choices monks individually made.

192
00:16:50,359 --> 00:16:53,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't, like, wake up one morning and suddenly became a fully ordained monk.

193
00:16:54,141 --> 00:16:54,321
No.

194
00:16:55,515 --> 00:17:01,599
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to leave the home, you had to leave the family, the, you know, the people you were involved, you had to.

195
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:05,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it can never be pleasant.

196
00:17:06,343 --> 00:17:06,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Ever.

197
00:17:08,804 --> 00:17:12,047
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize responsibility, you recognize the value of it.

198
00:17:12,827 --> 00:17:16,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just when you ignore that, then you just try to keep it, as you said, at the arm's length.

199
00:17:16,930 --> 00:17:19,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are lucky you're a monk and I'm unlucky.

200
00:17:19,592 --> 00:17:22,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, none of us is responsible for our own situation.

201
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:27,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Or when people say, oh, I can't be a monk now because I have the family to look after.

202
00:17:27,882 --> 00:17:28,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that is true.

203
00:17:29,543 --> 00:17:30,704
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the reason you can't be a monk.

204
00:17:30,764 --> 00:17:32,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But why do you have a family that you have to look after?

205
00:17:33,206 --> 00:17:35,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Was that just such a magically thrown upon you?

206
00:17:35,487 --> 00:17:38,289
[SPEAKER_00]: You just woke up one morning with six children you had to look after?

207
00:17:39,569 --> 00:17:39,790
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

208
00:17:40,350 --> 00:17:49,195
[SPEAKER_00]: You each time, individually, chose that to engage in a full-on sensual acts that result in children, sometimes planned or unplanned, doesn't matter.

209
00:17:49,716 --> 00:17:52,037
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot happen accidentally.

210
00:17:55,568 --> 00:17:59,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you have to look after the consequences of your actions.

211
00:18:00,032 --> 00:18:02,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's exactly what saṃsāra is.

212
00:18:05,216 --> 00:18:07,177
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what I mean.

213
00:18:07,537 --> 00:18:08,678
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, so what do you do?

214
00:18:08,979 --> 00:18:12,061
[SPEAKER_00]: What you do is stop ignoring responsibility for that.

215
00:18:12,541 --> 00:18:17,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll know whether you really want to be a monk or you're fine by staying a layman.

216
00:18:17,786 --> 00:18:23,230
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll never be clear on that for as long as you ignore responsibility for the situation you're already in.

217
00:18:28,714 --> 00:18:44,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And, say, someone wants to go forth or even to get a taste of, while staying a layman, what he or she should do.

218
00:18:45,688 --> 00:18:50,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you know precepts, like, what precepts entail before you commit to them?

219
00:18:50,412 --> 00:18:53,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you, like, this is the results I'll have.

220
00:18:54,533 --> 00:18:55,594
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no, no.

221
00:18:55,634 --> 00:19:05,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You can know them in terms of... you can know where they are and what they require you to abstain from and practice and be mindful of, but until you start doing it, it's...

222
00:19:05,761 --> 00:19:13,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, it's like asking, can you feel the heat of the fire that you would have lit up with these wet sticks if they were dry?

223
00:19:15,528 --> 00:19:22,813
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you can know that there will be a heat as a result of the fire, but you can't feel that heat for as long as you don't dry the sticks.

224
00:19:24,193 --> 00:19:35,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as long as you don't dry out your behavior of greed, aversion, delusion, conceit and everything else, you can't feel the heat of the dhamma in terms of that wisdom and that liberation from suffering.

225
00:19:37,661 --> 00:19:38,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to commit to it.

226
00:19:39,783 --> 00:19:39,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

227
00:19:39,983 --> 00:19:42,704
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to keep the precepts in order for your mind to start drying out.

228
00:19:43,745 --> 00:19:45,266
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the wisdom can take place.

229
00:19:45,886 --> 00:19:50,229
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot foresee what's the... You can foresee on the abstract level.

230
00:19:51,270 --> 00:19:51,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

231
00:19:51,630 --> 00:19:53,071
[SPEAKER_00]: The heat will probably be the result.

232
00:19:53,943 --> 00:19:59,387
[SPEAKER_00]: But, what that heat is, or how it's felt, and how it's experienced, you don't know, because your sticks were always wet.

233
00:20:02,970 --> 00:20:03,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

234
00:20:03,991 --> 00:20:08,194
[SPEAKER_00]: You might choose, then, that whatever your family, your committee, your job, is more important to you.

235
00:20:08,614 --> 00:20:13,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, rather, having the insecurity from giving these things up, is something you don't want.

236
00:20:15,139 --> 00:20:21,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And, on account of it, you say, well, this dhamma is all good, but, you know, I like being being a layman.

237
00:20:21,724 --> 00:20:22,685
[SPEAKER_00]: That's absolutely fine.

238
00:20:23,825 --> 00:20:29,188
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as you don't use that to at the same time cover up responsibility for that very choice, that's all.

239
00:20:30,088 --> 00:20:44,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't cover up the responsibility for that very choice, now you will feel responsible for not following the way out of suffering, for staying in the environment that provides you with pleasure you are familiar with, and provides you also with possibilities of suffering that you have to deal with.

240
00:20:45,737 --> 00:20:48,598
[SPEAKER_00]: And then endure that responsibility of that choice.

241
00:20:49,979 --> 00:20:52,060
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's actually what the Buddha was teaching to lay people.

242
00:20:52,719 --> 00:20:56,382
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say you should all become monks and nuns.

243
00:20:56,863 --> 00:21:00,126
[SPEAKER_00]: He said you should all be aware that you are the owners of your actions.

244
00:21:01,227 --> 00:21:03,208
[SPEAKER_00]: The burden of your choice is always on you.

245
00:21:03,308 --> 00:21:05,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, choose wisely, choose skillfully.

246
00:21:05,650 --> 00:21:12,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, even as a layman, choose according to the five precepts, as in not out of greed, aversion, delusion, don't harm others.

247
00:21:12,857 --> 00:21:15,179
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things, because it's the same principle.

248
00:21:17,397 --> 00:21:20,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the worst thing, basically, is ignoring the responsibility.

249
00:21:20,799 --> 00:21:22,059
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where ignorance comes first.

250
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:23,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the root of everything.

251
00:21:23,720 --> 00:21:23,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

252
00:21:23,900 --> 00:21:25,741
[SPEAKER_01]: You said, like, eh... familiarity.

253
00:21:26,021 --> 00:21:26,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

254
00:21:26,262 --> 00:21:31,044
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... earlier, that, eh... the simile you gave, like, you always had wet sticks.

255
00:21:31,264 --> 00:21:31,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

256
00:21:31,404 --> 00:21:32,225
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know anything else.

257
00:21:32,345 --> 00:21:33,345
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know anything else, yeah.

258
00:21:33,685 --> 00:21:35,546
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you have a degree of faith, initially.

259
00:21:36,547 --> 00:21:44,071
[SPEAKER_00]: To, like, recognize, abstractly, that, yeah, if the sticks dry out, it's more... greater likelihood that they'll light a fire.

260
00:21:45,038 --> 00:21:53,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now I just have to endure for as long as it takes for them to dry out, and maybe, you know, don't jump back into the water, because that was my familiar environment.

261
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:59,401
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will stop being your familiar environment, and the sense restraint will become your familiar environment.

262
00:22:00,641 --> 00:22:04,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will require your effort not to restrain, but to go back to centrality.

263
00:22:06,423 --> 00:22:08,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you know you're on the right path.

264
00:22:12,170 --> 00:22:12,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

265
00:22:13,151 --> 00:22:19,095
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason, like, giving things up, keeping precepts, accepting responsibility is unpleasant, is because you keep avoiding it.

266
00:22:20,216 --> 00:22:21,797
[SPEAKER_00]: You always have been avoiding it.

267
00:22:24,719 --> 00:22:28,181
[SPEAKER_01]: So, someone hears this, eh... starts with the sensual strain.

268
00:22:28,382 --> 00:22:28,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Hm.

269
00:22:30,363 --> 00:22:30,923
[SPEAKER_01]: What next?

270
00:22:32,825 --> 00:22:33,765
[SPEAKER_01]: How... how do you like that?

271
00:22:33,785 --> 00:22:35,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, we're still talking about the... the layperson.

272
00:22:37,041 --> 00:22:37,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even a monk.

273
00:22:37,942 --> 00:22:38,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Even a monk.

274
00:22:38,622 --> 00:22:38,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:22:39,442 --> 00:22:44,444
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start with a sense restraint as a necessary basis for understanding the dhamma.

276
00:22:45,164 --> 00:22:47,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the next step is then to start contemplating things.

277
00:22:48,405 --> 00:22:52,127
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you want to understand the dhamma?

278
00:22:53,167 --> 00:22:53,887
[SPEAKER_01]: To end suffering.

279
00:22:53,987 --> 00:22:55,348
[SPEAKER_01]: To not suffer.

280
00:22:56,368 --> 00:22:57,009
[SPEAKER_00]: To not suffer.

281
00:22:58,249 --> 00:23:00,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, on the basis of sense restraint,

282
00:23:01,597 --> 00:23:06,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you haven't been engaging with sensuality, haven't been giving into your will, expression of it.

283
00:23:06,980 --> 00:23:08,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is your suffering there?

284
00:23:15,565 --> 00:23:22,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, eh... let's say, you don't know, like... all your life, all you knew was sensuality.

285
00:23:23,349 --> 00:23:24,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you realize...

286
00:23:24,130 --> 00:23:25,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, think of it like this, for example.

287
00:23:25,431 --> 00:23:28,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you want something.

288
00:23:28,132 --> 00:23:29,193
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a sensual desire.

289
00:23:30,286 --> 00:23:31,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And you restrain yourself.

290
00:23:32,348 --> 00:23:33,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not doing it.

291
00:23:33,889 --> 00:23:35,551
[SPEAKER_00]: It's felt unpleasantly.

292
00:23:35,571 --> 00:23:38,293
[SPEAKER_00]: What is unpleasant?

293
00:23:38,333 --> 00:23:39,194
[SPEAKER_01]: The feeling.

294
00:23:40,695 --> 00:23:42,717
[SPEAKER_00]: The feeling of displeasure.

295
00:23:43,257 --> 00:23:49,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it unpleasant?

296
00:23:49,883 --> 00:23:54,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, well, it's pressuring.

297
00:23:54,647 --> 00:23:55,327
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sort of...

298
00:23:57,345 --> 00:23:57,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

299
00:23:57,645 --> 00:23:59,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is pressure, left and right.

300
00:23:59,888 --> 00:24:02,551
[SPEAKER_00]: But why is this pressure felt unpleasantly by you?

301
00:24:07,296 --> 00:24:10,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because you said no to yourself?

302
00:24:22,020 --> 00:24:24,602
[SPEAKER_01]: You would want to jump at that sort of a conclusion, but not really.

303
00:24:24,822 --> 00:24:25,023
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

304
00:24:25,743 --> 00:24:26,704
[SPEAKER_01]: It's...

305
00:24:26,764 --> 00:24:27,505
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is it then?

306
00:24:28,045 --> 00:24:29,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can say yes, it is.

307
00:24:29,186 --> 00:24:30,227
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you said no to yourself.

308
00:24:30,247 --> 00:24:31,268
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the most obvious answer.

309
00:24:31,508 --> 00:24:32,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

310
00:24:32,229 --> 00:24:33,490
[SPEAKER_00]: But why did you say no to yourself?

311
00:24:35,171 --> 00:24:35,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Was there nothing?

312
00:24:36,412 --> 00:24:40,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, suddenly, you just said no to yourself, and pain arisen as a direct result of saying no to yourself.

313
00:24:40,936 --> 00:24:44,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Or was there something before you said no to yourself, to which you said no?

314
00:24:45,039 --> 00:24:45,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

315
00:24:45,420 --> 00:24:45,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

316
00:24:45,580 --> 00:24:46,881
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to have something to say no.

317
00:24:46,901 --> 00:24:46,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah.

318
00:24:47,061 --> 00:24:49,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, your pain started before you said no.

319
00:24:53,533 --> 00:24:57,374
[SPEAKER_00]: You saying no was not the first point of that whole experience.

320
00:24:58,634 --> 00:25:06,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, when you... like, if saying no is where the pain is, means anytime you say no, it will be felt painfully.

321
00:25:08,276 --> 00:25:09,096
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the case.

322
00:25:09,877 --> 00:25:16,518
[SPEAKER_00]: If I offer you this poisonous drink, to drink it, they will cause you pain for weeks, and you say no to it?

323
00:25:16,818 --> 00:25:20,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you suffer on account of that, or would you be elated and happy on account of that very no?

324
00:25:22,378 --> 00:25:28,841
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be elated and happy, because the basis for saying no is what determines whether it's felt pleasantly or unpleasantly.

325
00:25:30,482 --> 00:25:40,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, when you say no to your sensual desire, and you suffer on account of it, that pain is in sensual desire, and saying no reveals the pain that started with sensual desire.

326
00:25:42,008 --> 00:25:49,012
[SPEAKER_00]: If sensual desire were truly pleasant, as I said that many times before, you wouldn't feel pressured towards it.

327
00:25:53,272 --> 00:25:59,095
[SPEAKER_00]: If sensual desire were pleasant, you wouldn't need to act out of it, because desire in itself would be bliss.

328
00:26:00,196 --> 00:26:01,077
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not, isn't it?

329
00:26:01,557 --> 00:26:08,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual desire means you need to act, and get, and obtain, and maintain, and protect, and engage with it more, and so on.

330
00:26:10,343 --> 00:26:10,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means,

331
00:26:11,379 --> 00:26:14,100
[SPEAKER_00]: It touches you painfully and you want to get rid of the pain.

332
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:20,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's always the first and always the the the main framework for any engagement with sensuality.

333
00:26:20,461 --> 00:26:22,922
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter the pleasure you can get secondarily from it.

334
00:26:23,422 --> 00:26:26,603
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always rooted first in trying to avoid the pain of it.

335
00:26:28,063 --> 00:26:32,444
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sense restraint can never be the reason for neither pain nor pleasure.

336
00:26:33,367 --> 00:26:36,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Abstaining from action cannot be the reason for pain nor pleasure.

337
00:26:37,130 --> 00:26:44,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only reveal the reason for pain or pleasure that's already implied in the action you're restraining from.

338
00:26:44,236 --> 00:26:49,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as you're already committing to saying no, not seeing... you're saying no to things.

339
00:26:49,700 --> 00:26:49,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

340
00:26:50,121 --> 00:26:53,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, you're going to blame your sense restraint for your pain.

341
00:26:53,423 --> 00:26:53,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

342
00:26:54,584 --> 00:27:01,710
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, if that were always... if that were truly the case, means sense... means saying no to your senses would always be unpleasant.

343
00:27:02,030 --> 00:27:02,551
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

344
00:27:03,587 --> 00:27:06,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to the harmful exposure of the senses is felt pleasant.

345
00:27:06,469 --> 00:27:08,311
[SPEAKER_00]: It's felt, oh, God, I dodged a bullet there.

346
00:27:10,412 --> 00:27:15,416
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same act of saying, saying no to, I refuse this sight, I refuse this taste.

347
00:27:18,959 --> 00:27:22,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, when you feel the pain of sensory strain, stop misidentifying it.

348
00:27:23,122 --> 00:27:28,166
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only stop misidentifying it, if you started practicing sensory strain first.

349
00:27:30,938 --> 00:27:33,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you should do, once you start practicing sense restraint.

350
00:27:34,622 --> 00:27:36,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Start seeing that the pain is not in that.

351
00:27:36,483 --> 00:27:38,405
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain is that which you're restraining yourself from.

352
00:27:42,710 --> 00:27:45,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how long do you have to...

353
00:27:45,272 --> 00:27:46,333
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no prescription.

354
00:27:48,896 --> 00:27:52,859
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said himself, sometimes half a night is enough, sometimes...

355
00:27:53,649 --> 00:27:56,970
[SPEAKER_00]: You maybe need to do a full day, something, seven days, seven months, seven years.

356
00:27:57,290 --> 00:28:00,851
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, if you're doing it like this, the results will have to come.

357
00:28:02,012 --> 00:28:05,093
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be doing it rightly without the right results.

358
00:28:05,353 --> 00:28:06,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Results that are freedom from suffering.

359
00:28:06,954 --> 00:28:07,994
[SPEAKER_00]: That is inconceivable.

360
00:28:09,054 --> 00:28:11,095
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another thing you should factor in.

361
00:28:11,115 --> 00:28:12,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you need to be patient to some extent.

362
00:28:14,196 --> 00:28:19,958
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, at the same time, don't pretend, don't think like, oh, so there's ever, never any verifiable result for me.

363
00:28:21,550 --> 00:28:24,632
[SPEAKER_00]: I just need to do this out of faith and there is nothing to be attained anyway.

364
00:28:24,692 --> 00:28:25,552
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there is.

365
00:28:25,572 --> 00:28:26,593
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a lot to be attained.

366
00:28:27,653 --> 00:28:29,774
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not attaining it, it's because you're not doing things rightly.

367
00:28:31,515 --> 00:28:32,176
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

368
00:28:32,896 --> 00:28:34,957
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't believe me, well, read the suttas.

369
00:28:36,238 --> 00:28:44,302
[SPEAKER_00]: On many occasions they would have said, in many different ways, but always the best simile was the hen sitting on her eggs.

370
00:28:46,243 --> 00:28:47,904
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if the hen wishes,

371
00:28:48,749 --> 00:28:54,970
[SPEAKER_00]: May I be enlightened, or may my chicks pierce out safely into this world from these shells.

372
00:28:55,671 --> 00:28:57,511
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't, because she's not sitting on the eggs.

373
00:28:58,811 --> 00:29:03,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if the monk wishes, may I reach enlightenment, he won't, because he's not doing the right practice.

374
00:29:05,433 --> 00:29:16,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the other hen, she sits on her eggs, and even if she doesn't wish, may my chicks pierce out the shell safely and come into this world, they will, because she's sitting on the eggs.

375
00:29:17,096 --> 00:29:17,956
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the

376
00:29:19,060 --> 00:29:29,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Similarly, if the monk is doing the practice correctly, even if he doesn't wish, may my mind be purified from greed, urge and delusion, may I stop being liable to suffering, he will, because he's doing it rightly.

377
00:29:33,236 --> 00:29:37,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you hear people saying there's nothing to be attained, it's because they haven't attained anything and they're covering it up.

378
00:29:39,962 --> 00:29:45,247
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's nothing to be attained, why does every... virtually every sutta talks about attainments?

379
00:29:46,889 --> 00:29:50,692
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, having desire for attainments is unpleasant.

380
00:29:52,834 --> 00:29:55,417
[SPEAKER_00]: And people don't want this pleasure on account of their dhamma practice.

381
00:29:56,277 --> 00:29:59,200
[SPEAKER_00]: They want soft, inspirational, poetic words.

382
00:30:00,121 --> 00:30:03,044
[SPEAKER_00]: They will make them feel good, not feel bad.

383
00:30:04,915 --> 00:30:16,730
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's literally like you go to the doctor and you ask for the cure and he says, yeah, it's bitter, it's gonna make you vomit, have diarrhea, wet your bed, torture you, but it will cure you and then you'll be fine.

384
00:30:16,870 --> 00:30:18,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, but I don't want all the side effects.

385
00:30:19,594 --> 00:30:21,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you just continue pretending you're not ill.

386
00:30:34,977 --> 00:30:36,939
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think, that's where he started, isn't it?

387
00:30:36,959 --> 00:30:39,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, being practical.

388
00:30:39,601 --> 00:30:40,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Giving you the arm's length, yeah.

389
00:30:42,363 --> 00:30:52,992
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's what I mean, like, you can... you won't even see the value of being practical and clear in understanding and the meaning, for as long as you're avoiding your own personal responsibility.

390
00:30:56,495 --> 00:30:57,396
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just...

391
00:30:58,720 --> 00:31:05,405
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to stop all the responsibility for what you choose to be, to do, to attend.

392
00:31:06,966 --> 00:31:08,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get a better picture.

393
00:31:08,747 --> 00:31:10,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you'll be unable to ignore as much.

394
00:31:16,573 --> 00:31:21,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I said, yeah, wanting to be free from suffering is yet another desire.

395
00:31:21,857 --> 00:31:26,280
[SPEAKER_00]: But as the Buddha said, it's the desire that can then start directing you

396
00:31:27,067 --> 00:31:31,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards finding out what the dhamma is, which will then eventually result in freedom from desire.

397
00:31:32,289 --> 00:31:36,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's desire, that will eventually cancel all the other desire, including itself.

398
00:31:36,690 --> 00:31:42,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what Buddha meant when he said, desire is to be abandoned by the means of desire, and craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving.

399
00:31:42,892 --> 00:31:45,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You crave for liberation from suffering.

400
00:31:45,753 --> 00:31:46,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And craving is suffering.

401
00:31:48,883 --> 00:31:57,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But because of craving for liberation, not craving for sensuality and everything else, you actually get to make the effort, discern, layer by layer, what liberation is.

402
00:31:57,386 --> 00:32:00,566
[SPEAKER_00]: And by doing so, you free yourself from the very craving that made you do it.

403
00:32:02,247 --> 00:32:03,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a good craving and bad craving.

404
00:32:05,288 --> 00:32:06,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Good craving will cancel itself.

405
00:32:07,349 --> 00:32:08,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Bad craving will perpetuate itself.

406
00:32:12,171 --> 00:32:23,454
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't see either, if you keep avoiding and turning a blind eye to the responsibility of your actions, and your situation, and your choices, and all the results that come your way as a result of where you chose to be.

407
00:32:24,654 --> 00:32:32,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha compared the whole situation of staying in saṃsāra, like being in the house that the roof's on fire, and it's collapsing bit by bit, and it will crush you.

408
00:32:33,436 --> 00:32:35,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And he compared enlightenment to leaving that environment.

409
00:32:36,297 --> 00:32:41,298
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't compare enlightenment to repairing the roof beams, or re-roofing the tiles.

410
00:32:42,903 --> 00:32:44,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is usually what people want dhamma to do.

411
00:32:45,024 --> 00:32:49,507
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to fix my life so nothing bothers me, but also I can still stay in the house.

412
00:32:49,527 --> 00:32:49,707
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

413
00:32:49,867 --> 00:32:51,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma takes you out of your life.

414
00:32:52,088 --> 00:32:52,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of existence.

415
00:32:53,349 --> 00:32:53,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Overcomes it.

416
00:32:54,789 --> 00:32:57,431
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you leave the house that is on fire.

417
00:32:58,031 --> 00:32:59,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Not you repair it or extinguish the fire.

418
00:33:00,232 --> 00:33:02,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You leave the dangerous environment.

419
00:33:02,974 --> 00:33:03,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You do dhamma.

420
00:33:04,235 --> 00:33:05,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Or choose not to.

421
00:33:05,295 --> 00:33:07,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can't complain when you get burnt.

422
00:33:08,357 --> 00:33:12,239
[SPEAKER_00]: A little bit, a bit more, and then eventually, when the whole roof starts coming down.

423
00:33:13,828 --> 00:33:19,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, most of the time, it's like, you do the dhamma, so you can get back to what you were...

424
00:33:19,771 --> 00:33:28,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I do the dhamma as a way of, kind of, psychological psychotherapy, dealing with things that currently bother me, but only so that I can return to things that don't bother me.

425
00:33:29,116 --> 00:33:35,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that whether there are things bothering you or not bothering you, either way, you're affected by things, and that's where the problem is.

